what you might remember back in June  Clark County Commissioners voted to pass  some new regulations on short-term  rentals now those changes caused massive  concern for hosts all across the valley  in fact so much so that one Association  sued the county claiming several of the  regulations were unconstitutional while  on Monday the district court agreed with  them so what's next Madison Kimbrough  spoke with those involved and joins us  now with the details  many short-term rental hosts feeling  relieved tonight after the ruling  stating that with the regulations in  place many feared that they would have  to shut down and that was on the minds  of many  well you know I'm breathing a little  easier because I think we really needed  to get that injunction short-term rental  host and the greater Las Vegas  short-term rental Association feeling  Victorious after the district court  granted them preliminary injunction  against the county in regards to their  regulations on short-term rentals passed  in June the County's ordinance states  that properties must allow County  inspections without notice and hosts  must enter a lottery system in order to  obtain a short-term rental license large  Gatherings of more than 10 people would  not be allowed reservations must have a  minimum of a two-night stay rentals must  also be a thousand feet away from each  other these regulations not sitting well  with host and Clark County as many feel  it's keeping them from running a  successful business and making a decent  income it's just totally blown out of  proportion of the county you know then  it's not fair  you know why why can't I make money off  my room there's people that are simply  trying to make ends meet there's people  trying to put their kids through college  there's people that have elderly parents  that are now sick and need to be put in  Assisted Living short-term rentals are a  great way to make that income necessary  Jackie Flores with glv s t r a says with  the District Court's decision to go back  and review these regulations it makes  her hopeful for the future of short-term  rental hosts in Clark County it feels  really good that finally we have you  know the judge agreeing with us in what  we're saying so now the Dutch is  ordering uh the county and us to work  together to fix the issues with the  ordinance  and the county still doesn't have an  exact date to when they will be giving  out licenses but for those coming into  town for the holiday the current  regulations still apply now in order to  apply for a license here in Clark County  the deadline is in March reporting live  Madison Kimbrough 8 News Now
 

